 "text": "Here's how to buy your first ETF step by step. I'm gonna show you exactly what I tap to place my ten dollar order. First, pick a brokerage. You can use Robinhood, Fidelity, or Vanguard. I use Robinhood, so that's what you're seeing on my screen. Once your account is open, go to the home screen and tap buying power. Tap add money. Enter how much you wanna transfer, and link your bank if you haven't already. Then review the transfer and confirm it. Once the money is available to invest, tap search. VOO, SPY, IVV, and SPYM are four ETFs that track the S&P 500. Think of the S&P 500 like a list of about 500 big US companies like Apple and Microsoft. One ETF can give you exposure to those companies in a single purchase. You can pick any one of those four to get exposure to the same index. I'm using VOO for this example. So boom. I search VOO, open the Vanguard S&P 500 ETF, then tap trade and buy. I entered a fraction of a share. See that estimated cost right there? Mine came out to ten dollars and one cent. I double-checked the amount and the account, then swiped up to submit. See where it says order queued? Mine was waiting for the market to open. Once the order fills, that's when I own that fraction of a share. If you wanna keep investing, you can set up a recurring buy every week or every month for an amount that fits your budget. And real quick, I'm not a financial advisor. I'm showing you what I did. Always do your own research before you buy anything. Save this for when you're ready to place your first order, and send it to that friend who keeps asking, \"How do I start investing?\"",
